A||||2||theogre||roof rattles.||||10||
Z||000000||theogre||04-02-2000||12:21 AM||||Is your roof rattling? Read on.<P>Sunroof.<P>Note: There are 2 versions of sunroof hinge tab. The original version is plastic coated. The later version is not. This article is written based on only the uncoated tabs.<P>For some time now my roof has rattled when opened. Everything is adjusted and tight. The problem occurred mostly in the left hinge. My roof uses the uncoated hinge tabs. They are only painted.<P>Recently I got fed up with it and yanked the glass out. I checked and the hardware was slightly loose. I tightened it but still got the rattle. All the hardware is good, all tight. So what’s left.<P>I took out the glass again and looked at the hinge pockets. I thought maybe it was loose. On closer inspection I noticed the front edge of the pocket was bowed toward the front of the car. This just didn’t seem right. So I carefully straightened it with a small screw driver. ((WARNING: If you pry hard you will likely damage something. You do not need allot of force. Note: There is some "float" in the hinge pockets. As far as I can tell it is supposed to be like that.))<P>Since straightening the pocket the roof has stopped rattling on all but to really big bumps.<P>How did the pockets get bowed? By binding the hinge tabs taking the glass out and in most likely. It doesn’t take much to bend them, and you can generate allot of force with the glass.<P>Main roof section rattles/creaks.<P>There is a GM TSB on this. The hardware that holds the roof panel and the sunroof can come loose. GM recommends pulling the head liner and taking the nuts off the roof panel. Apply Locktite to the threads and reinstall the roof bolts. GM says the sunroof nuts have thread lock on them. Just retorque them. There are 5 nuts for the main roof, 6 for the sunroof.<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000001||lowCG||04-02-2000||12:52 PM||||My next assault on rattles will be in the door area; hinges,and latches.<BR>Any ideas here?||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||04-02-2000||05:27 PM||||hinges:<P>replace the bushings and pins. (Motormite red Help! packages. you want the short GM ones) and a nylon washer. The washer goes in the top of the lower hinge. <P>The bushings don't support the vertical load on the pins well. the washer fixes that. You will likely need to adjust the bottom hinge to compensate for the thickness of the washer. The adjustment requires removing the inner door skin.<P>I also use wheel bearing or silicone brake grease on these. it stays put better.<P>if you can't adjust the stike pin to compensate for latch wear then you'll need to replace the latch.||||reg||1
